<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SH806 — the suite logs to a console it can discard, and to no file at all.

  ## The failure this exists for

  One run of the Java gate reported 8 errors over 1856 tests, none of them an assertion. Three
  @SpringBootTest classes failed to start and the rest was Spring's failure-threshold cascade:

      Logging system failed to initialize using configuration from 'null'
      ERROR in SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy - Timeout while waiting for clean-up job to finish

  The next run was green, 1894 of 1894, with the string absent — a false red, which is the shape
  SH755 says teaches people that red means nothing, and which SH800 made worse by recording it: the
  stamp said `red: true` and the comparators then demand a red-suites.json entry for a defect nobody
  can find.

  ## What it actually was

  `logback-spring.xml` includes Spring Boot's `file-appender.xml`, whose LOG_FILE falls back to
  `${java.io.tmpdir}/spring.log`. Tests never set `logging.file.name` — `src/test/resources/`'s own
  `application.properties` shadows production's, where that key lives — so **every Spring context in
  the suite opened a rolling file appender in the system temp directory**, and its rolling policy ran
  a clean-up job over that directory looking for old archives to delete.

  Measured on this machine: `%TEMP%` holds **309,064 entries**, and `spring.log` had grown to 3.2 MB
  with a fortnight of gzipped archives beside it. A clean-up scanning that, from dozens of contexts,
  on a machine already running the suite, is the timeout.

  ## Why a file rather than a property

  `logging.file.name=` in the test properties would leave `logback-spring.xml`'s own LOG_FILE default
  in place, since that default is expressed in the XML and not in Spring's property binding. Logback
  and Spring Boot both prefer `logback-test.xml` over `logback-spring.xml`, so this replaces the
  configuration outright — which is also the honest description of the intent: a suite has nothing to
  roll, nothing to archive, and no business writing into anybody's temp directory.

  The console half is copied from `logback-spring.xml` deliberately, loggers and pattern included, so
  what a test prints is unchanged and no assertion about output moves.
-->
<configuration>
	<appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
		<encoder>
			<Pattern>
				%msg%n
			</Pattern>
		</encoder>
	</appender>
	<logger name="com.viglet.shio.Shio" level="info" additivity="false">
		<appender-ref ref="CONSOLE" />
	</logger>
	<logger name="com.viglet.shio.onstartup.ShOnStartup" level="info" additivity="false">
		<appender-ref ref="CONSOLE" />
	</logger>

	<include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml" />
	<root level="INFO">
		<appender-ref ref="CONSOLE" />
	</root>
</configuration>
